---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';

// Copy accurately describes the product (claim-verified against source
// 2026-08-10; the storage sentence was tightened during verification —
// "Nothing you scan or download is stored", because the operator blocklist
// IS stored and open-sourcing is on the deferred list) but is NOT legally
// reviewed — the Phase 7 legal-review item covers it. The
// automation-prohibition line is deliberately verbatim despite the FAQ's
// softer "not yet" API framing: both define the service as
// interactive-use-only, and the prohibition is what lets a limit-evader be
// refused on policy rather than debated on mechanism. If an API ever
// ships, this line and the FAQ change in the same commit (STATUS).
const lastUpdated = __TERMS_LAST_UPDATED__;
const dateHuman = new Date(`${lastUpdated}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
});
---

<Layout
	title="Terms of use — ImageExtract"
	description="ImageExtract's terms of use: what the tool does, your responsibility for what you download, prohibited uses, site opt-out, and takedown requests."
>
	<SiteHeader anchorPrefix="/" nav={false} />
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-content px-md py-xl">
		<article class="terms-article">
			<h1 class="text-h1 font-semibold text-text">Terms of use</h1>
			<p class="mt-md text-body text-muted">
				ImageExtract is free to use, with no account. By using it you agree to these terms.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What the tool does</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				You give it the address of a public web page. It reads that page's HTML, lists the images
				referenced there, and lets you preview, filter, and download them.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				It doesn't create images, own them, or grant you any rights to them. It finds addresses
				that are already published on a page and fetches them on your behalf.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">
				Your responsibility for what you download
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				The images belong to whoever made them. Finding an image with this tool gives you no
				right to use it.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Copyright, trademark, licensing, model releases, and every other restriction apply
				exactly as they would if you had saved the image from your browser. Working out what
				you're allowed to do with a file is your responsibility, not ours, and we have no way to
				know.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				If you're not sure whether you can use something, assume you can't.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">What you may not use it for</h2>
			<ul class="mt-sm list-disc pl-lg text-body text-muted">
				<li>Downloading material you have no right to download</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">
					Redistributing images in ways their rights holders haven't permitted
				</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">Getting at content behind a login, paywall, or other access control</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">
					Circumventing technical measures a site uses to restrict access
				</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">Automating the service, scripting it, or reselling access to it</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">Anything illegal where you are</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				We enforce per-network request limits to keep the service usable. Don't work around
				them.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Sites can opt out</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Site owners who don't want their pages scanned can write to
				<strong class="text-text">support@imageextract.pics</strong> and have their domain
				excluded. See <a href="/traffic" class="text-accent hover:underline">/traffic</a> for
				what the tool's requests actually do.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Takedown requests</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				If material accessible through this tool infringes your copyright, write to
				<strong class="text-text">support@imageextract.pics</strong> with:
			</p>
			<ul class="mt-sm list-disc pl-lg text-body text-muted">
				<li>What the work is</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">The URL where it appears</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">A statement that you own the rights or act for the owner</li>
				<li class="mt-xs">Your contact details</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				We don't host images. Nothing you scan or download is stored on our servers, so there is
				no copy for us to remove — but we can exclude a domain from the tool entirely, and we'll
				do that where a claim is credible.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				Where the material is on a site we don't control, the takedown belongs with that site or
				its host. We can only stop our tool from reaching it.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">No warranty</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				The service is provided as it is. It may be unavailable, may fail to find images on a
				page, may return images you didn't expect, and may change or stop at any time without
				notice.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				We don't guarantee that it works, that results are complete, or that it's fit for any
				particular purpose.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Limitation of liability</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				To the extent the law allows, we aren't liable for any loss arising from your use of the
				service — including consequences of what you download or how you use it.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-xl text-h3 font-semibold text-text">Changes</h2>
			<p class="mt-sm text-body text-muted">
				If these terms change, the date below changes with them. Continuing to use the service
				means accepting the version in effect.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-sm mb-xl text-caption text-muted">Last updated: {dateHuman}</p>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	/* Reading measure: half the content width, derived from the container
	   token rather than an invented value (the .hero-form precedent). */
	.terms-article {
		max-width: calc(var(--container-content) / 2);
	}
</style>
